# Core standards

## Philosophy

- **Radical simplicity for users**: reduce cognitive friction; every flow should feel obvious.
- **Minimize diff scope**: fix the requested problem only; do not refactor, rename, or “improve” unrelated code.
- **Avoid over-engineering**: no one-line helpers, no premature abstractions, no extra error handling for impossible edges.
- **Match the codebase**: read surrounding files first; new code should look like the same author wrote it.
- **Human-in-the-loop**: when AI or automation is involved, keep review/edit paths visible and trustworthy.

## Implementation

- Prefer extending existing components and utilities over parallel implementations.
- Comments only for non-obvious business logic — not for self-explanatory code.
- Tests only when they cover real behavior; skip trivial assertions.
- Run build/lint/tests yourself; do not ask the user to verify what you can run locally.
- Only commit or push when explicitly asked.

## Communication

- Write like a clear technical blog post: complete sentences, plain language, proportional length.
- Use code citation blocks (`startLine:endLine:filepath`) when referencing existing code.
- Do not create markdown docs the user did not ask for.

## Stack defaults (adapt per project)

- React (functional components) + Vite + React Router
- Tailwind CSS v4 via `@import 'tailwindcss'`; domain styles in separate CSS files
- Plain JS/JSX unless the project uses TypeScript
- `node:test` + `node:assert/strict` for unit tests when tests exist
- Supabase for auth/data when backend is needed
